  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x² + 0.5 z²)/2, drawn at half its value with contour lines. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first. With no noise, steps diverge along x for learning rates above 0.8 (2/2.5).",
    "Noise is Gaussian with mean zero, centred on the true slope, with sd 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, so it is exactly zero at 64. Each step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "The arrows are steps, not gradients: orange = learning rate × noisy slope, negated (where the ball moves next); grey = learning rate × true slope, negated. Both are drawn at true length (scale 1).",
    "A run stops after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged when the ball leaves the drawn square or the loss goes past 10. The ball is then shown at the bowl's edge, and the loss readout still gives the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed sequence, so Reset replays it from the start. A new run carries on with the next values. Five steps a second at 1× speed."
  ]
}